This weakens the prosecution case further.

If the injuries later relied on in Ben were not noted by nurses when he arrived at the hospital, that raises additional uncertainty about:
- whether the injuries were clearly present and observable at the relevant time,
- whether some were subtle, later-developed, or artifacts,
- and more generally how robust the medical interpretation really is.

This does not prove the injuries were absent or meaningless—nurses are not pathologists, and some internal findings would not be visible on admission. But in a case already turning on contested forensic significance, the failure to note injuries at the time of presentation reduces confidence.

Taking the evidence cumulatively as now developed:
- the coincidence/similarity evidence is suspicious but not strong on its own;
- Andy’s injuries are consistent with resuscitation;
- several of Ben’s originally suspicious findings have been explained away or weakened;
- Ben lacked some typical smothering signs;
- and now the injuries later emphasized were not noted on hospital arrival.

What still remains suspicious:
- Ben’s hypoxic brain damage and eye/eyelid hemorrhages were said to be consistent with smothering or violent trauma;
- there was an opinion that some hypoxic injury predated death by hours;
- Jane was alone with both babies at the critical times.

Even so, from this summary, the case now looks too uncertain and medically contestable to make guilt more likely than not with confidence. My estimate drops somewhat further.

